    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>, who called themselves Ebodalo on their coins, were militarily important from 450, when they defeated the Kidarites, to 560, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">First Turkic Khaganate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> destroyed them.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peroz-i">Peroz I</a> fought three wars against them from 474: captured and ransomed twice, he was defeated and killed at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in 484, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> paid tribute until the 530s.</li><li>They were a tribal confederation of nomadic and settled communities who adopted the Bactrian language, took Sasanian coin designs, and ruled a largely Iranian population through local rulers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Polyandry was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>' most noted custom: brothers shared one wife, whose children belonged to the eldest, and the number of horns on a married woman's headdress showed how many husbands she had.</p></aside>
    </section>

