    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/peroz-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peroz-i.jpg" alt="Peroz I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/peroz-i">Peroz I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_of_Peroz_I_hunting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hermitage Museum, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> belong to what the numismatist Robert Göbl called the Iranian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>. Their stronghold was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a>, on the northern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> in today's southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, with a capital probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunduz">Kunduz</a>. Their origins are disputed: the Weishu, as quoted in the Tongdian, says they were a branch of the Gaoju, early Turkic speakers, who came south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> around 360, and the most prominent current view, that of de la Vaissière, makes them Turkic in origin, adopting the Bactrian language after settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>; others have argued for Iranian or Proto-Mongolic roots, and Richard Frye called them 'probably a mixed horde'.</p><p>They were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate</a> until the early 5th century, borrowing the title khan, before moving south-east to escape Rouran pressure. The Hephthalites became a significant power in Bactria around 450, displacing the Kidarites and then the Alchon Huns, who went on into Gandhara and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Persian sources name them as foes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-ii">Yazdegerd II</a> from 442. Their first embassy reached the Northern Wei in 456. In 458 the king Akhshunwar helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peroz-i">Peroz I</a> take the Sasanian throne from his brother Hormizd III, and by 467 Peroz, with Hephthalite aid, had captured Balaam and ended Kidarite rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>. A tax receipt from the Kingdom of Rob dated 483/484 securely attests Hephthalite rule in Bactria.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sogdians"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sogdians.jpg" alt="Sogdians" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sogdian_Tribute_Bearers_on_the_Apadana_Staircase_16_(Best_Viewed_Size_%22Large%22)_(4689076272).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A.Davey from Portland, Oregon, EE UU, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> were a tribal confederation that included both nomadic and settled urban communities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> insisted they were 'not nomads like the other Hunnic peoples' but settled under one king and a lawful constitution, while some Chinese sources said they lived in tents; Litvinsky sees nomads who moved into the cities they conquered. They adopted Bactrian, an Eastern Iranian language written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a>, as their official tongue and inscribed their coins with it, calling themselves Ebodalo. Documents from the Kingdom of Rob record taxes paid to Hephthalite rulers between 484 and 527.</p><p>Wealth came above all from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. After 474 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> paid 'an enormous tribute' until the 530s, and Peroz's coinage flooded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a>; the Hephthalites developed no coinage of their own but minted Sasanian designs or countermarked Sasanian coins with their symbols. The ransoms and tributes may have been reinvested in Sogdia, which they conquered in 479 or 509 and which grew extremely prosperous as the centre of a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> with the Sasanian and Byzantine empires; the Hephthalites took over the Kushans' old role as intermediary and contracted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a> to carry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and luxury goods. They may have built fortified grid-plan cities at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. De la Vaissière estimates the major oases of Tokharistan and western Turkestan at several hundred thousand people each, those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> at tens of thousands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kavad-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kavad-i.jpg" alt="Kavad I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kavad-i">Kavad I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_with_king_hunting_rams_(white_background).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">None, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> were a powerful military force whose main weapon, depending on the source, was the bow, the mace or the sword; the Chinese considered them excellent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, and their achievements imply a strong cavalry. The Armenian chronicler Lazar Parpetsi wrote that even in peacetime the mere mention of a Hephthalite terrified everybody in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. The reliefs of the Bandian complex seem to show a defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> in 425 and then alliance, until the Hephthalites destroyed the complex in 484.</p><p>From 474 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peroz-i">Peroz I</a> fought three wars against them. In the first two he was captured and ransomed; after the second he handed over thirty mules loaded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> drachms and left his son Kavad as a hostage. In the third, the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in 484, he was defeated and killed by the king Kun-khi, and for two years the Hephthalites plundered and controlled the eastern Sasanian Empire. In 488 a Hephthalite army beat the Sasanian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balash">Balash</a> and enthroned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-i">Kavad I</a>, and in 498 the king gave Kavad 30,000 troops to regain his throne in return for Chaganiyan; Joshua the Stylite records Kavad leading Hephthalite troops against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at Theodosiupolis in 501–502 and Edessa in 503. In the east they took the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> from the weakening Rouran, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> in 479 and the Urumchi region in 497–509. The two-point sword suspension and the lamellar helmet spread from them to the Sasanians, and a dagger of 'Hunnic' design found at Gyerim-ro reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan.jpg" alt="Buddhas of Bamiyan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan">Buddhas of Bamiyan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddha_of_Bamiyan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James Gordon, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rule rested on a confederation of local rulers and governors bound by alliance agreements. Central control was weak: there were some government officials, but local dynasties paid tribute, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> such as Asbar of Vardanzi minted their own coins. The ruler bore the title yabghu, as on the seal reading 'Ebodalo Yabghu', the Lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>, dated to the late 5th or early 6th century. Known rulers are few: Akhshunwar around 458, Kun-khi around 484, Yilituo Yandai around 516, Hwade-gang from the Rob archive, Ghadfar around 567–568 and Faghanish of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghaniyan">Chaghaniyan</a>. There is no king-list.</p><p>Government and religion were mixed. Chinese sources say the Hephthalites worshipped the heaven god or the fire god, and the monk Song Yun, who visited in 540, reported that they did not recognise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>; some destroyed Buddhist monasteries, which others rebuilt. Yet under their dominion Buddhism was predominant, with Zoroastrian and Manichaean minorities and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> adherents; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> had some 100 monasteries and 30,000 monks, the capital of Chaghaniyan five, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan">Buddhas of Bamiyan</a> were begun under Hephthalite rule. In 549 they asked the patriarch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>, Mar Aba I, to consecrate a priest they had chosen as their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Cities such as Balkh, Kobadiyan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> were allowed to send their own embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the Hephthalite envoys led the column of foreign ambassadors at the Liang court in 516–526.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khosrow-i.jpg" alt="Khosrow I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_of_the_Sasanian_king_Khosrow_I_Anushirvan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For nearly a century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> held the balance of power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. They made and unmade Sasanian kings, raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peroz-i">Peroz I</a> in 458 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-i">Kavad I</a> in 488 and 498, and from 474 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> paid them tribute until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a>'s rise in the 530s. Their conquest of Sogdia placed them at the centre of a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Sasanian and Byzantine empires; Sasanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> received as tribute spread along it, and the Hephthalite symbol appears on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>'s coinage from 500 to 700. Chinese chronicles record twenty-four embassies between 456 and 558.</p><p>Their rule unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> culturally. The 'Hephthalite period' in art, with caftans folded to a triangular right lapel, cropped hair and crowns with crescents, appears in the murals of Balalyk Tepe and Tavka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a>, among the donors painted on the ceiling of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan">Buddhas of Bamiyan</a>, in Sogdia and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizil-caves">Kizil Caves</a> of Kucha; their rulers also fill the panels of the Sogdian trader Wirkak's tomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>. A number of peoples have been proposed as their descendants, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a>, whom Gankovsky connects with the dissolution of the Hephthalite confederacy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalaj">Khalaj</a>, the Abdals and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clans; Chinese chronicles still mention a Hephthalite kingdom in 748.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-i">Kavad I</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> turned their attention away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> (531–579) resumed expansion to the east. In 552 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">First Turkic Khaganate</a>. Around 555–567, perhaps in 557, the Turks and Khosrow I allied against the Hephthalites and defeated them after an eight-day battle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarshi">Qarshi</a>, the Battle of Gol-Zarriun. The empire fragmented into semi-independent principalities paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> or the Turks as the military situation dictated; the defeated Hephthalites withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and replaced king Gatfar with Faghanish, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghaniyan">Chaghaniyan</a>, upon which Khosrow crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> and put Chaghaniyan and Khuttal under tribute. The Oxus became the frontier between the two empires.</p><p>The remnants lasted long. Principalities in Chaghaniyan, Khuttal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/termez">Termez</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, Badghis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> held castles on the roads to Bamiyan, where the giant Buddhas were built around 570 and 618. The Hephthalites rebelled against the Sasanians on Khosrow's death in 579 and were crushed by the Turks; they joined Bagha Qaghan's invasion south of the Oxus in 588 and raided as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> around 600 and 616–617. From 625 the Western Turks took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a> as the Tokhara Yabghus. After the Arab conquest of Balkh in 652 the principalities paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and Nezak Tarkan's revolt of 709 ended in 710 when Qutaiba ibn Muslim captured and executed him despite a promise of pardon.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>, the White <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a>, rose around 450 to dominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> from Sogdia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peroz-i">Peroz I</a> in 484, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-i">Kavad I</a> king and drew tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> for sixty years. A Turkic-Sasanian alliance destroyed their empire around 560, but Hephthalite principalities on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> survived into the 8th century.</em></p>

